2186441034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441110 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e48890ff-d63d-57c7-b77b-86bd5ac0b261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441111 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb58af87-7a8d-591a-a5c6-db3582a4b58b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=75,$ where $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,75.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a412ed0-e9f7-5318-a109-33611797297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441112 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a population of size $N=75,$ and $p=0.8$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=75(0.8)=60,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=75(1-0.8)=15.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second member does not have the characteristic given that the first member does not have the characteristic either.\n\nIf the first member does not have the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=75-1=74$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=14$ members remaining who do not have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{14}{74}=\\frac{7}{37}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that  $8%$ of a particular university's student population attends the law school. A random sample of $n=100$ students is taken from the population and asked which school they attend.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who attend the law school? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d5c4439-72cc-5a29-8805-27f9db8f5589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441113 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=8%=0.08,$ and $n=100.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =8%=0.08, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.08(1-0.08)}{100} \\\\  & ≈0.0007\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1071 "A bag contains a large number of equally-sized buttons, of which $15%$ are white. A random sample of $n=18$ buttons is taken from the bag.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of white buttons in the sample? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of buttons in the bag is significantly larger than the number in the sample.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e62b0fb-05ae-5348-8fa8-26e1b9d8b974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441114 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation 