t{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=22%=0.22,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =22%=0.22, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.22(1-0.22)}{25} \\\\  & ≈0.0069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=400$ from a population where $32%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,400.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{400}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "669ea571-4e73-5129-bf0b-c10dd97639ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441117 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{