dom variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=48%=0.48,\\,n=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(180,0.48).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1071 "A box contains $70$ marbles, of which $30%$ are red and the rest are green. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th marble in the box is red} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,70.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6b56e16-5d64-5848-860d-3d44e0d9f335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441120 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a population of size $N=70,$ and $p=0.3$ represents the proportion of the red marbles.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of red marbles equals \n\n$$\nNp=70(0.3)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of green marbles equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=70(1-0.3)=49.\n$$\n\nWe select two marbles at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second marble is green given that the first marble is also green.\n\nIf the first marble is green, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=70-1=69$ marbles remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=48$ marbles remaining that are green.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{48}{69}=\\frac{16}{23}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441121 #edb/ref 1071 "Sup