d}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441123 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a population of size $N=60,$ and $p=0.35$ represents the proportion of the population of left-footed children.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of  left-footed children equals\n\n$$\nNp=60(0.35)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of right-footed children equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=60(1-0.35)=39.\n$$\n\nWe select two children of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second child is left-footed given that the first child is right-footed.\n\nIf the first child is right-footed, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=60-1=59$ children remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np=21$ children remaining who are left-footed.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{Np}{N-1}=\\frac{21}{59}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
